course: arts
lesson: 27
title: Theatre: stage, actors, backstage
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Theatre is a story told live, right in front of you: the actors are really there, nothing can be re-filmed, and every night is a little different.
explain: Behind the stage is a hidden team: the stage crew, dressed in black so you hardly notice them, move scenery, run the lights and sound, and help actors change costumes in seconds.
explain: Theatre does not try to hide everything; in The Lion King musical you see the people under the animal puppets, and your imagination does the rest, like when you play "this sofa is a pirate ship".
explain: Theatre happens anywhere, from a huge London theatre to a tiny room above a pub, and some shows want silence while a panto wants you to shout "He's behind you!".
explain: People say it their own way: "babe" is a friendly word for anyone, in Ireland "like" is tacked on the end of a question, and "heckle" means shouting comments at a performer.
fact: Pantomime, or panto, is a British Christmas show where the audience is meant to shout, boo the villain and cheer; most other plays expect a quiet audience.
fact: The stage crew work backstage, often dressed in black so the audience does not notice them.
fact: Stage actors learn to project, which means speaking loudly and clearly using breath, not screaming, so the back row can hear.
fact: In The Lion King musical the puppets are designed so you see both the actor and the animal.
fact: Shakespeare wrote his plays from about 1590 to 1613; they were popular fun for rich and poor alike, and poorer audiences at the Globe in London stood in front of the stage, ate snacks and could be noisy.
fact: "Break a leg" is a theatre way of saying good luck, because many actors think saying "good luck" brings bad luck; nobody knows exactly where it came from.
fact: Some theatres are tiny rooms, and some plays are "in the round", with the audience on all sides.
fact: When an actor forgets a line, other actors can cover by making something up, and a prompter may whisper the line from the side.
fact: Theatre can help people understand others' lives; since 2022 many theatres around the world have staged plays by and about Ukrainians.
fact: Big shows in London's West End or New York's Broadway can be expensive, but afternoon shows called matinees, day seats and local theatres can be cheaper.
exercise: Ellie is shocked the audience shouts "He's behind you!". Why is that allowed? | Panto wants you to join in | Every play in the world wants the audience to shout as loudly as possible | The actors on stage cannot see the villain, so the audience has to warn them | Shouting is always banned
exercise: Who are the "ninjas" in black backstage? | The stage crew | Actors waiting in the dark for their big sword-fight scene to start | Security guards who stop people from stealing the props and costumes | Ushers who sell ice creams and programmes to the audience at the interval
exercise: Why do theatre actors speak so loudly? | So everyone up to the back row can hear | Because they are cross with the audience | Because there is always a microphone fault | So the actors next door can hear
exercise: What does it mean to project your voice? | Speak loudly and clearly using your breath | Scream as hard as you can | Speak into a projector | Whisper so people lean in
exercise: Ming can see the people under the Lion King puppets. Why is that fine? | Your imagination fills in the rest | The puppets were broken that night and the actors had to show themselves | It is a mistake the theatre forgot to fix before the show opened | Lions were too expensive
exercise: Liam says Shakespeare is "ancient". What was a Shakespeare audience like? | Poorer people stood, ate and heckled | Everyone sat in total silence, dressed in their finest and fanciest clothes | Only kings, queens and lords were ever allowed through the doors to watch | They watched from boats
exercise: Ali hears "Break a leg!" before the show. What does it mean? | Good luck | I hope you fall over | Watch out for the stairs | Stop acting badly
exercise: Why do actors say "break a leg" instead of "good luck"? | "Good luck" is thought unlucky | Legs are the most important part of acting, so actors wish them strong | It is an old curse from a famous play that actors still repeat for fun | It wishes a fall
exercise: Saoirse's play is in a room above a pub. Which is true? | Theatre can happen in small rooms | It cannot be real theatre without a big stage and red velvet curtains | Plays are only allowed by law to be performed inside proper theatres | Pubs are for rehearsals
exercise: An actor forgets a line and another covers it. What is that? | Making something up | Ruining the whole play for everyone who came to see it | Stopping the show for the rest of the night and sending people home | Asking someone in the audience to shout out the missing line
exercise: What does a prompter do? | Whispers a forgotten line from the side | Sells the programmes | Turns the lights off | Plays the villain in the panto
exercise: Mariia, a refugee from Kharkiv, cries at a play about Ukraine. What can theatre do? | Help people share hard experiences | Only make people laugh and forget their troubles for an evening | Make people forget everything that ever happened to them | Nothing, plays are games
exercise: Ruby's mum says the matinee is cheaper. What is a matinee? | An afternoon show | A seat right on the stage | A show with no actors | A play only for children
exercise: What is the West End? | London's main theatre area | The back row of a theatre | A part of the stage | A famous theatre in New York
exercise: What is "in the round"? | A stage with the audience on all sides | A play performed on a roundabout | A show that goes on all day | A stage shaped like a square
exercise: When did Shakespeare write his plays? | From about 1590 to 1613 | In the 1900s | About 3,000 years ago | Last century in America
test: Your mate goes, "Theatre's for posh people with loads of money, innit." What is the best answer? | Cheap seats and local shows exist | He is right, every single theatre ticket costs a huge amount of money | Only kings, queens and very rich people are allowed to go to the theatre | Kids are not let in
test: What would happen if you shouted "He's behind you!" at a serious quiet play? | People would be annoyed | Everyone in the audience would join in, just like at a panto | The play would change into a panto halfway through to suit you | The actors would stop and thank you for being so helpful
test: A kid from New England says the backstage crew are "wicked fast" ("wicked" means very). What do they do? | Run scenery, lights, sound and costumes | They only clean the toilets and sweep the floor after each show | They play extra parts on stage whenever an actor is ill | They sell tickets
test: In a cartoon, a theatre actor whispers all his lines and the audience leans forward. What should he do? | Project his voice so the back row hears | Whisper even more softly | Wait for a microphone to be fixed | Leave the stage until people stop leaning
test: Your cousin says Shakespeare was "dead boring even back then". Which is true? | His plays were popular fun | Nobody at all came to see his plays when he was alive | His plays were only ever read in books, never acted on a stage | They were only for kings
test: Somebody shouts "Break a leg, mate!" at your little brother before his show. He gets upset. What do you tell him? | It is a friendly way of saying good luck | They really want him to get hurt | It means he will forget his lines | It is an insult from Shakespeare's time
test: An actor dries (forgets a line) and his partner says something to cover. Owen says "nobody noticed, bro covered it". Which is true? | Actors help each other and keep going | One forgotten line ruins the whole play for the whole audience | The show must always stop and start again from the very beginning | The actor is sacked
test: A Gen Alpha kid says The Lion King puppets are "kinda sus, I can see the guy" ("sus" means suspicious). What is the reply? | You are meant to see both | The theatre forgot to hide the actors properly under the costumes | Real trained animals are used in the second half of the show | The puppets are broken in every show and nobody has fixed them
test: Which is true about where plays happen? | Almost anywhere | Only in big old buildings with red curtains and gold balconies | Only in school halls at the end of the summer term | Only in the big cities of London and New York, nowhere else
test: Why might a refugee clap louder than anyone at a play about her country? | The play helped her feel understood and share what happened | She did not understand the play | Clapping louder is a rule for refugees | She was told to clap by the actors
test: A Dublin kid says, "Where's the stage, like?" in a tiny room. What is going on? | The play is close to the audience | The play has been cancelled and nobody told the audience | The actors forgot to bring the stage with them that night | It is a radio play
exam: Identify the principal reason for which theatrical performers are trained to project their voices. | So that even the most distant seats may hear | So that the performers may express their displeasure at an inattentive audience | So that microphones may be dispensed with in every theatre without exception | To frighten the audience
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the expression "break a leg" as employed in the theatre? | A customary wish of good fortune prior to a performance | A threat directed at a rival performer | A warning concerning hazardous stairs | A curse originating in a play by Shakespeare
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning audiences at Shakespeare's Globe. | Poorer spectators stood and could be noisy | All spectators were seated in comfort and remained wholly silent throughout | Admission was restricted by royal order to members of the royal family | Admission was always free
exam: Ascertain the conduct expected of the audience at a British pantomime. | Shouting, booing the villain and cheering are expected | Complete silence throughout the performance is required | Spectators are forbidden to respond in any way | Spectators are required to perform upon the stage
